WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.630
“Hi, I’m from the school’s IT

2
00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:05.130
team, and I just need your

3
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.910
password to fix your account.”

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.860
That’s what a caller told one

5
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.320
of my students last term.

6
00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:14.310
He was polite, he knew her

7
00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.970
name, and he sounded busy.

8
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.490
She almost told him.

9
00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.710
Today: social engineering, how

10
00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.170
attackers trick people instead of

11
00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.690
computers, and how you stop them.

12
00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:31.770
Breaking into a

13
00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.810
well-protected computer is hard.

14
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.000
Tricking a person is often much easier.

15
00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.530
That’s social engineering: getting someone

16
00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:44.210
to hand over a password, a code, money or

17
00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.490
access, by playing on their feelings.

18
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.900
Attackers use the same few

19
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:51.720
tricks again and again.

20
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.620
Authority: “I’m from IT, the

21
00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:56.860
bank, the ministry.”

22
00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.330
Urgency: “Do it in the next ten minutes.”

23
00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.750
Fear: “Your account will be banned.”

24
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:06.970
A gift or flattery: “You’re

25
00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:07.890
our lucky winner.”

26
00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.090
And curiosity: a file or a

27
00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:12.830
stick you just have to open.

28
00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.130
It happens everywhere.

29
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.960
A phone call from “support”.

30
00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.170
A message in a game chat

31
00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:21.770
from a “moderator”.

32
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.910
A new account using a friend’s photo.

33
00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.460
Or someone who follows you into the

34
00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.360
computer room because their hands are

35
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:29.520
full.

36
00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.270
Think of a pickpocket in the

37
00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:33.990
market who asks you the time.

38
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.440
While you look at your watch, his

39
00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.160
partner reaches for your bag.

40
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.300
Social engineers work the same way:

41
00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.240
they keep your mind busy with a

42
00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.280
feeling, so you don’t stop to think.

43
00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:47.010
Quick check!

44
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:50.110
You find a USB stick in the computer

45
00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.810
room, labelled “Exam answers”.

46
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.040
What do you do?

47
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.430
Hand it to a teacher, unopened.

48
00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.480
The label is the bait: curiosity

49
00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.540
is exactly what the person who

50
00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:03.740
left it is counting on.

51
00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.490
A planted stick can install malware

52
00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.150
the moment it’s plugged in.

53
00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.400
One defence works against every trick.

54
00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:16.010
Slow down: pressure is a warning sign.

55
00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.810
Check through another channel: call

56
00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.110
back on a number you already know, or

57
00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:22.530
ask a teacher face to face.

58
00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.450
Never share passwords or codes;

59
00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:27.730
real support never needs them.

60
00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:31.030
And remember, “no” is always allowed.

61
00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.840
How do you tell real help from a trick?

62
00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:37.310
Real help lets you take your

63
00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:38.670
time and call back.

64
00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:42.250
It never asks for your password or a code.

65
00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.550
A trick rushes you, threatens you,

66
00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:47.830
and wants a secret right now.

67
00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:51.090
In the lab, you’ll take a call from

68
00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.190
“IT support” about a virus.

69
00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.720
Choose what you’d really say, and see

70
00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.480
which tricks the caller used on you.

71
00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:00.360
Your turn.

72
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.720
In a game chat, a “moderator” says your

73
00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.040
account will be banned in ten minutes

74
00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.280
unless you confirm your password.

75
00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.460
Real or a trick?

76
00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:17.740
A trick: a deadline, a threat and a

77
00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.840
password request, all in one message.

78
00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:22.370
Remember: feel the pressure,

79
00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:25.490
pause, check another way, and

80
00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.890
never share a password or a code.

81
00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.090
Try it tomorrow: tell someone at

82
00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.210
home about these tricks.

83
00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:36.470
Agree on one family rule: nobody shares a

84
00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:38.590
code or a password, whoever asks.

85
00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.240
And if a call feels wrong, hang up and

86
00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.880
call back on a number you know.
